# Composer components

This is the admin contract for a page block. The palette, the canvas, and the props panel all read it. The phone and the web client are separate apps; they only share the saved `type` and the `values` keys.

## Layout

```
components/
  types.ts              shared ComposerComponent shape
  register.tsx          turns a config into a palette item and a canvas renderer
  PropsFromFields.tsx   builds the props form from fields
  ComposerProps.tsx     fields form + spacing/background, used when a block has no custom Props
  PlaceholderPreview.tsx  empty canvas state for blocks that are not built yet
  <category>/
    registry.ts         list of blocks in that menu
    index.tsx           registers the list
    <block>/
      config.ts         the definition
      Preview.tsx       canvas render, when it is not a one-line placeholder
      Props.tsx         custom props form, only when fields are not enough
```

Each category `index.tsx` calls `registerComponents`. Importing `@/modules/composer/components` loads every category, which fills the shared `components` list the canvas and props panel look up by `type`.

## Definition

A block is one `ComposerComponent` object:

| Field | Role |
|---|---|
| `type` | Stored on the module as `data.type`. Lookup key. Never rename a type that is already saved. |
| `label` / `desc` | Palette card. |
| `category` | Which side-menu group lists it: `ui_elements`, `text`, `products`, `carousel`, `categories`, `animations`. |
| `placement` | `body` is a normal stacked row. `chrome` configures an existing shell piece (header, and later footer). The admin still shows it as a row so it can be previewed. |
| `singleton` | When true, a page can hold only one row of this `type`. |
| `defaults` | Copied into `values` when the block is dropped. |
| `fields` | Simple props. Each `key` is stored at `values.<key>`. Types: `switch`, `text`, `number`, `select`, `color`. |
| `fieldsTitle` | Heading above those fields. Defaults to "Value". |
| `Preview` | Canvas component. It receives `{ item }`. Read `item.values` and `item.styles`. |
| `Props` | Optional custom props panel. Use this when the form needs a picker, a list, or a schedule. If omitted, `ComposerProps` renders `fields` and the shared styling fields. |
| `serialize` | Optional. Runs on save and returns the object stored in `values`. Use it to trim a value down to ids. |

`Preview` and `Props` receive the form row:

```ts
{
  name,    // field path, e.g. "rows[0]". Props bind inputs to `${name}.values....`
  values,  // the block content
  styles,  // padding, margin, background
  status,  // "online" | "offline"
  schedule_start, schedule_end
}
```

## What gets saved

Dropping a block stores only identity on `data`: `{ type, label, desc }`. React components stay in the registry and are looked up again by `type`.

`values` is saved as a JSON string. `styles` is the shared spacing and background object. `sort_order` is the row index. `status` defaults to `online`.

## Add a block

1. Create `<category>/<block>/config.ts` and export a `ComposerComponent`.
2. Add that export to the category `registry.ts`.
3. Give `Preview` a real component when the placeholder is no longer enough.
4. Leave `Props` unset while `fields` can describe the form. Add `Props.tsx` when the form is custom, and include `ComponentStyling` (and `ComponentSchedule` if the block can be scheduled) inside that panel.
5. Add `serialize` only when the stored value should differ from the form value.

The side menu, the drop handler, and the props window do not need a change for a new block in an existing category.

A new side-menu group needs a new `category` value, a folder with `registry.ts` and `index.tsx`, an export from `components/index.ts`, and one entry in `sideMenu.tsx`.

## Simple block vs custom props

Use `fields` when each control maps to one `values` key (a heading, a switch, a select).

Use `Props` when the form has its own state: product picker, repeating slots, file upload, schedule. A custom `Props` replaces the generated form, so it must render styling itself.

`defineText` and `definePlaceholder` are helpers for those two common cases. A block that outgrows them should replace the helper with its own `Preview` and, if needed, `Props`.
